---
import Adr from "../../components/Adr.astro";
import Excerpt from "../../components/Excerpt.astro";
import Layer from "../../components/Layer.astro";
import Layout from "../../components/Layout.astro";
import PageLink from "../../components/PageLink.astro";
import Path from "../../components/Path.astro";
import Rule from "../../components/Rule.astro";

const WH = "server/src/iki_server/webhooks";
const NOTE = "server/src/iki_server/notifications";
const RT = "server/src/iki_server/realtime";

const H = {
  short: "Events say that something changed, not what it says",
  how: "Who hears of a change, and what they're told",
  rules: "Webhooks, notifications and event streams",
  ref: "Code and decisions",
};
const toc = [
  { id: "short", label: H.short },
  { id: "how", label: H.how },
  { id: "rules", label: H.rules },
  { id: "ref", label: H.ref },
];
---

<Layout toc={toc}>
  <Layer n={1} title={H.short}>
    <p>
      Webhooks, notifications and live-update streams tell people and systems that something changed.
      They carry identifiers and counts, never a record's values: whoever receives one reads the
      values through the API, with their own access (<Adr n={[30, 31]} />). Each is checked against
      access when it's sent, and again where it can be: a webhook an app added before every attempt,
      a notification in the inbox and before its email, a stream event before it leaves the server
      (<Adr n={[129, 152, 159]} />). How events are made and delivered is on <PageLink
        to="Tasks and live updates"
      />.
    </p>
  </Layer>

  <Layer n={2} title={H.how}>
    <div class="table-wrap">
      <table>
        <thead><tr><th>Channel</th><th>Who receives it</th><th>What it carries</th><th>Checked</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr>
            <td>Project webhook</td>
            <td>A URL its project's Admins chose</td>
            <td>The project's events: identifiers, the actor and the agent; positions and counterparts outside the project hidden</td>
            <td>When queued and before every attempt</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Organization webhook</td>
            <td>A URL Org Admins chose</td>
            <td>Every event, with the same identifiers</td>
            <td>When queued and before every attempt</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Notification</td>
            <td>Active people the type names who can read the record</td>
            <td>The type's message over the record's values, in Iki only</td>
            <td>When made, when the inbox is read, before the email</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Email</td>
            <td>The notification's recipient</td>
            <td>What kind it is, which record and where, and a link</td>
            <td>Before sending</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Event stream</td>
            <td>One signed-in person or token</td>
            <td>That something changed: IDs and counts</td>
            <td>Before each event, and every 20 seconds</td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p>A webhook names a record by its identifiers alone:</p>
    <Excerpt file={`${WH}/events.py`} start="^def entity_data\(" />
  </Layer>

  <Layer n={4} title={H.rules}>
    <div class="rules">
      <Rule title="Webhooks send identifiers" open>
        <p>
          Events are built from the audit log, in the change's transaction, so a webhook message
          exists exactly when its change does. An entity's payload is its identifiers, plus the actor
          and the agent (<Adr n={[31, 72]} />, <Path p={`${WH}/events.py`} sym="def from_audit(" />).
        </p>
        <p>
          A project webhook's copy is narrowed to its project: a storage move keeps an endpoint only
          when the project can read its container, and an amount movement's counterpart outside the
          project is <code>unreadable</code> (<Path p={`${WH}/events.py`} sym="def project_movement(" />,{" "}
          <Path p={`${WH}/events.py`} sym="def project_amount_movement(" />). Org Admins see where each
          project's webhook sends, its host only, and can turn any of them off; its deliveries carry
          project data, so they're its managers' (<Path p={`${WH}/services.py`} />).
        </p>
      </Rule>
      <Rule title="An app's webhook lasts as long as its access">
        <p>
          A webhook added with a token sends only while the app could still add it: its person's
          consent and roles as they stand. That's checked as each message is queued and again before
          every attempt, including retries. When it no longer holds, Iki turns the webhook off and
          tells its managers. One a person added in the browser is theirs, and always may send (<Adr
            n="0152"
          />, <Path p={`${WH}/services.py`} sym="def held(" />, <Path
            p={`${WH}/tasks.py`}
            sym="if not services.may_send(webhook):"
          />).
        </p>
      </Rule>
      <Rule title="Notifications go to those who can read the record">
        <p>
          A notification names the record whose change it tells of (<Adr n="0159" />). At send time
          each candidate must be active and a person, not a service account or an imported placeholder;
          able to read the record, and for a change to a record it refers to, that one too; not muted
          or opted out; and not whoever made the change by hand, unless the type says so. An agent's,
          run's or trigger's change tells the person it was made for (<Adr n="0129" />, <Path
            p={`${NOTE}/declared.py`}
            sym="access.readers(record, people)"
          />).
        </p>
        <p>
          Each firing is audited as <code>notification.sent</code>, with whom it told and whom it
          didn't and why, so a lab can answer "was QA told?".
        </p>
      </Rule>
      <Rule title="The inbox and email check again">
        <p>
          The inbox shows a notification only while its person can still see its project and read its
          record, and a token limited to some projects sees only theirs (<Path
            p={`${NOTE}/services.py`}
            sym="def inbox("
          />). An email says what kind of notification it is and which record, never the record's
          values, and is sent only if the inbox would still show it (<Path
            p={`${NOTE}/tasks.py`}
            sym="def headline("
          />, <Path p={`${NOTE}/tasks.py`} sym="no longer theirs to see" />).
        </p>
      </Rule>
      <Rule title="Event streams check before each event">
        <p>
          A stream event is addressed to one person or a topic, such as a run, and carries IDs and
          counts. Before sending each one, the stream checks the session or token again as a request
          would, and what the caller may see: a notification only if their inbox shows it, a run's
          events only while they can read the run, a setup build's only for Compute Admins. A
          credential that's no longer valid ends the stream with <code>signed_out</code> (<Adr
            n={[30, 152]}
          />, <Path p={`${RT}/views.py`} sym="Check = Callable[[], bool]" />, <Path
            p={`${RT}/views.py`}
            sym="async def _prepare("
          />).
        </p>
      </Rule>
    </div>
  </Layer>

  <Layer n={5} title={H.ref}>
    <div class="refs">
      <h3>Where</h3>
      <ul>
        <li><Path p={`${WH}/events.py`} />: the events and their payloads</li>
        <li><Path p={`${WH}/services.py`} />: who may send, and to whom</li>
        <li><Path p={`${NOTE}/declared.py`} />: types' notifications and their recipients</li>
        <li><Path p={`${RT}/views.py`} />: the live-update streams</li>
      </ul>
      <h3>Decisions</h3>
      <ul>
        <li><Adr n="0030" title /></li>
        <li><Adr n="0031" title /></li>
        <li><Adr n="0129" title /></li>
        <li><Adr n="0152" title /></li>
        <li><Adr n="0159" title /></li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </Layer>
</Layout>
